<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="integrations-google-workspace"
      updated="July 31, 2026"
      evidence="Guide reviewed July 31, 2026."
      toc={[
        {"sso", "Single sign-on"},
        {"scim", "Directory sync"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="integrations-google-workspace">
        <:dek>
          Connect Google Workspace to emisar: an Internal OAuth client and a locked, prefilled
          issuer. Google has no outbound SCIM — the directory-sync section covers the gap.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This is the shortest provider setup because the issuer is prefilled.
      </p>
      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          A <strong class="text-zinc-300">Google Cloud project</strong>
          in the same organization as your Workspace.
        </:item>
        <:item>An emisar <strong class="text-zinc-300">owner or admin</strong>.</:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso">Single sign-on</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Connecting an identity provider is a paid feature. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>emisar</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Start the connection.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open
              <a
                href={~p"/app/sso/new"}
                class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
              >Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</a>
              and choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Google Workspace</strong>. Leave the page open: the
              callback URL on it is what Google asks for next, and Google's values come back here
              at the end.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-emisar-add-provider.webp"
              title="emisar · Add provider"
              caption="Picking the provider here tailors the rest of the form to it."
              alt="emisar's Add provider form with the Provider type select outlined in green and set to Google Workspace."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Google Cloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Name the app your team will see.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open <strong class="text-zinc-200">Google Auth Platform → Branding</strong>. The
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">App name</strong>
              and <strong class="text-zinc-200">User support email</strong>
              appear on Google's consent screen. Use a name your members recognize. On a new
              project, Google presents the same fields in the numbered setup shown next. The
              fields are identical.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-branding.webp"
              title="Google Auth Platform · Branding"
              caption="Both values appear on the consent screen your members see."
              alt="The Branding page of Google Auth Platform with App name and User support email outlined in green, above App logo and App domain sections."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Google Cloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Keep the audience Internal.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              On <strong class="text-zinc-200">Audience</strong>, the user type must be <strong class="text-zinc-200">Internal</strong>. That restricts sign-in to your own
              Workspace domain and skips Google's verification review entirely. External allows any
              Google account. Do not use it for emisar. Google rejects outside accounts with
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">org_internal</code>
              before the request reaches emisar.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-audience.webp"
              title="Google Auth Platform · Audience"
              caption="Internal is the whole domain restriction. Google refuses outside accounts itself."
              alt="Step 2, Audience, of Google's project configuration, with the Internal user type selected and outlined in green above the External option."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Google Cloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Give Google a contact address, then create.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              For a new project, complete <strong class="text-zinc-200">Contact
              Information</strong> and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Finish</strong>. Enter an
              address Google uses for project notices, then select <strong class="text-zinc-200">Create</strong>. Both fields are required but do not
              control sign-in. The Audience setting already controls sign-in. An existing project
              does not show these fields. Go straight to Clients.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-contact-information.webp"
              title="Google Auth Platform · Contact Information"
              caption="Google writes here about the project, not about sign-ins."
              alt="Step 3, Contact Information, of Google's project configuration, with the Email addresses field outlined in green and Finish below it."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            5
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Google Cloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Start an OAuth client.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open <strong class="text-zinc-200">Clients → Create client</strong>. Existing clients are
              listed here too, so this is where you come back to if you need the client ID again.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-clients.webp"
              title="Google Auth Platform · Clients"
              caption="Also where an existing client's ID is read from later."
              alt="The Clients page of Google Auth Platform with the Create client button outlined in green above a table of OAuth 2.0 Client IDs."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            6
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Google Cloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Make it a web application, with emisar's callback.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Set Application type to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Web application</strong>. Put
              emisar's callback under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authorized redirect URIs</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_value
              label="Authorized redirect URI"
              copy_text="https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback"
            >
              https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback
            </.docs_value>
            <p class="mt-4">
              Mind which list it goes in — <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authorized JavaScript
              origins</strong>
              sits directly above it and is not part of this flow. Google matches the URI
              exactly, so a trailing slash or
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">http</code>
              instead of <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">https</code>
              is rejected at sign-in with a <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">redirect_uri_mismatch</code>, not
              at save time.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-web-application.webp"
              title="Google Cloud · Create OAuth client ID"
              caption="The callback belongs under Authorized redirect URIs, not the JavaScript origins above it."
              alt="The Create OAuth client ID form with Application type set to Web application and the Authorized redirect URIs heading both outlined in green, and emisar's callback filled into URIs 1."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            7
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>Google Cloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Copy both values before you close the dialog.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Google shows the client ID and secret once. You cannot read the secret again. Copy
              both now or create another client later.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-client-created.webp"
              title="Google Cloud · OAuth client created"
              caption="The secret is shown once. Both values are redacted here; yours are real."
              alt="The OAuth client created dialog with Client ID and Client secret each outlined in green, both showing redacted placeholder values, above a warning that the secret cannot be viewed again."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            8
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>emisar</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Paste the credentials into emisar.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Pick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Google Workspace</strong>
              as the provider type and paste the client ID and secret. The
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Issuer URL</strong>
              is already filled in and locked —
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">https://accounts.google.com</code>
              for every Google customer — so it is one less thing to get wrong. Leave the identifier
              claim on <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/google-emisar-connection.webp"
              title="emisar console · Add provider"
              caption="Picking Google Workspace locks the issuer to https://accounts.google.com — the padlock means there is nothing for you to look up or mistype."
              alt="emisar's Add provider form with Provider type set to Google Workspace, the in-app setup steps naming the Google Cloud Console path, the redirect URI to register with a copy button, and a locked Issuer URL field showing https://accounts.google.com."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="The rest of the connection form">
        <p>
          This guide covers only the provider-specific fields.
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Single sign-on</.link>
          explains provisioning, default roles, runner access, and provider
          MFA settings.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="scim">Directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Google Workspace cannot push users to emisar — it provisions only applications in its
        SaaS catalog, and the custom OAuth client created above has no SCIM connector, so
        emisar hides the sync panel. Members are created at their first sign-in with the
        connection's default role, or held for administrator approval with Manual
        provisioning, and nobody is removed automatically.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Offboarding is therefore two steps: suspend the Google account to block new sign-ins,
        then suspend or remove the member in
        <a
          href={~p"/app/team"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team</a>
        to end their sessions and revoke their API keys.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="Want automatic deprovisioning?">
        <p>
          Route Google through an IdP that does have outbound SCIM — Okta, Entra or JumpCloud —
          and connect <em>that</em>
          to emisar. An offboard in Google then reaches us as the standard
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-950 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">active: false</code>
          signal and access is revoked without anyone touching emisar.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Federating sign-in through that IdP is not by itself a lifecycle feed. The IdP must manage those people through provisioning or
          synchronization. Its directory then tells emisar when a person leaves. Most organizations
          that federate Google already sync their staff into the same
          IdP, so there is usually nothing new to set up.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>
      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If the absence of Google directory sync is a deal-breaker for you, <a
          href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.sales()}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >contact our sales team</a>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
